<section id="instagram-threads-access">
<h2>Instagram and Threads access</h2>
<p>Fallback search found 27 distinct Instagram and Threads post URLs. It supplied 16 indexed excerpts and 11 titles without descriptions. Direct page requests could not recover full captions or video playback. No publication dates or platform engagement were visible in these results. The lessons below trace a few excerpts to primary technical sources. <a href="data/instagram-threads-search.json">Inspect the candidate and access record</a></p>
<p>A Reel excerpt on scripted workflows mentions skills and hooks without an event name. A Threads title on elaborate workflows omits its conclusion. These are leads for further reading. Neither supports a rule about when to invoke a hook or how large a workflow should be. <a href="sources.html#DI14">[DI14]</a> <a href="sources.html#DI15">[DI15]</a></p>
<p>The Edits bridge author provides a demo video and a written walkthrough. Use these to inspect the demo when the asset loads. The linked demo is a repository asset. The mechanism below uses the written walkthrough and protocol reference. No video frames or audio were examined. <a href="sources.html#DI07">[DI07]</a></p>
</section>

<section id="social-evidence-channels">
<h2>Keep the caption, the frame and the implementation separate</h2>
<p>The public <code>reels-to-md</code> skill separates the post caption, transcript and images from the video. It uses transcript timestamps to locate the matching frames, then emits a guide and scripts. Those outputs can contain steps the assistant inferred. This study read the procedure and skipped its optional browser-cookie step. <a href="sources.html#DI01">[DI01]</a></p>
<p>This separation matters when a reel claims that an agent can test a patch in parallel. The caption might name a product. A screen frame might show two terminal windows. Neither observation establishes that both commands tested the same source revision. Record each piece in a separate field. Find a primary artifact that settles the missing claim.</p>
<table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Evidence channel</th><th scope="col">Synthetic example</th><th scope="col">Testable conclusion</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Post caption</td><td>“Run two checks in parallel.”</td><td>The author proposes parallel checks.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Screen frame</td><td>Left prompt shows <code>feature/import</code>. Right prompt shows <code>main</code>.</td><td>The two terminal prompts name different branches.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Repository and run receipts</td><td>One receipt names the candidate revision. The second names the base revision.</td><td>Only one receipt covers the candidate.</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p>Try this with the repository workshop. Give a reviewer two compact receipts for one candidate. Each should show <code>command</code>, <code>revision</code>, <code>exit</code> and the affected test. Set the unit check to exit 0 on the candidate. Give the UI check exit 0 on the base. Require the reviewer to reject the second receipt. Run that check on the candidate to fill the gap. A third terminal window would add no evidence by itself. <a href="workshop-engineering.html">Repository workshop</a></p>
<p>Use one line per observed detail. Mark a command as partial if the cursor or caption covers its flags. If the source is a caption without a visible command, write a new local example and label it as yours. A search result that quotes the title can nominate a source. It cannot supply the missing command arguments.</p>
</section>

<section id="social-parallel-replay">
<h2>Check shared resources and replay boundaries</h2>
<p>James Goldbach's indexed Reel description proposes separate worktrees. Boris Cherny's indexed Threads excerpt names <code>--worktree</code>. The Claude Code documentation confirms the separate checkouts. Another Threads excerpt raises resource pressure when several agents build at once. It supplies no timing data. <a href="sources.html#DI10">[DI10]</a> <a href="sources.html#DI11">[DI11]</a> <a href="sources.html#DI12">[DI12]</a> <a href="sources.html#DI16">[DI16]</a></p>
<p>For the repository workshop, make both checks request port <code>4173</code> and separate checkout paths. The checkout names pass the Git isolation check. The port allocation fails. Give the second check port <code>4174</code> and a separate database or browser profile. Record the revision and runtime identity in each receipt. Measure one serial and one parallel run with the same inputs before you assume a time benefit.</p>
<p>Claude's indexed Threads announcement identifies dynamic workflows. The full product documentation explains one restart boundary. If a task fails in the middle of the observed sequence, a relaunch can reuse the completed prefix and repeat later tasks. A completed task after the failure can run again. <a href="sources.html#DI09">[DI09]</a> <a href="sources.html#DI13">[DI13]</a></p>
<p>Use three local steps for a briefing. Collect source IDs, validate the records and prepare a draft. Set the saved states to complete, failed and complete. The relaunch plan reuses the source IDs and runs both later steps again. Give draft creation a stable key and check that the retry produces one committed draft. A step with a public side effect needs a recorded result and a duplicate check before it can safely repeat. <a href="workshop-automation.html">Durable workflow workshop</a></p>
</section>

<section id="social-changing-tools">
<h2>Refresh a tool list when the application changes state</h2>
<p>The Edits bridge author reports that a direct Claude Code connection kept the tools available at initial connection. The editor offered other tools after the author opened a project. The bridge checks the upstream list after a tool call, compares the names and emits <code>notifications/tools/list_changed</code> when the names change. The MCP specification documents that notification for servers that declare <code>listChanged</code>. <a href="sources.html#DI02">[DI02]</a> <a href="sources.html#DI03">[DI03]</a></p>
<p>Build a fake server with two states. At home it exposes <code>open_project</code>. After the project opens it also exposes <code>preview</code>. Have a client save its first <code>tools/list</code> response. Omit the notification once. The client can open the project and still lack a usable preview tool. Add the notification and confirm that the second list adds <code>preview</code>.</p>
<p>Now use a harder case. Leave the name <code>preview</code> unchanged. Require a <code>revision</code> in the second input schema. Compare a list of sorted tool names with a digest of sorted tool definitions. The first misses this change. A local Python check verified that a digest over canonical JSON changed with the schema and stayed stable when the definitions were reordered. This schema case is a separate test from the bridge author's report. <a href="data/instagram-threads-search.json">Synthetic check results</a></p>
<table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Transition</th><th scope="col">Required client behavior</th><th scope="col">Fault to inject</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Home to project</td><td>Fetch the new list and see <code>preview</code>.</td><td>Drop the list-change notification.</td></tr>
<tr><td>First to second project schema</td><td>Require the stated revision before preview.</td><td>Keep names and count identical.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Project to closed state</td><td>Remove preview or return an explicit unavailable result.</td><td>Leave a stale tool card visible.</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p>For the durable briefing workshop, use the same pattern after source validation. Before validation, the runner can show status and find the source. After it validates the snapshot, allow a local draft. Put the draft revision in the tool input and reject a revision mismatch at the server. Refreshing a list helps the client choose an action. The server check protects the state when another call changes it first. <a href="workshop-automation.html">Durable workflow workshop</a></p>
<p>From the guide folder, run <code>python3 studies/social-tool-fixture.py</code>. It checks revision and port conflicts, replay decisions, catalog transitions, bounds and rates. It uses no network or browser. <a href="studies/social-tool-fixture.py" download>Download the local fixture</a></p>
<pre><code>Only the candidate check counts. Separate worktrees can request the same port.
Replay reuses the prefix and repeats the completed step after a failure.
Refresh exposes preview. Name-only comparison misses the schema change.
Schema digest detects the change. Stale and closed preview calls are rejected.
Bounds: fits, clips. Rates: 2%, 12%, unknown when exposures are absent.</code></pre>
</section>

<section id="social-preview-and-measurement">
<h2>Check the artifact at the surface where a person sees it</h2>
<p>The Instagram Skill author describes separate file validation and rendered preview stages. The preview shows the grid, the feed and individual slides. The author also describes a per-account plan and a local example for a fictional account. Repository docs and the author article supply the walkthrough. We did not run the publishing integration. <a href="sources.html#DI04">[DI04]</a> <a href="sources.html#DI08">[DI08]</a></p>
<p>Try the principle with a research answer card. Put the answer and source ID in the safe area of a 100-unit design. Define the safe region as rows 35 through 90. In the first layout, the source label spans rows 36 through 44. In the second, it spans rows 31 through 48. The local bounds check detects where the second layout overlaps the overlay. The fixture computes coordinates. Use an actual preview for the visual check.</p>
<p>Check the content twice. First compare the draft's source ID to the evaluator's manifest. Then open the final preview at the target width. Confirm that the link remains readable and clickable. A test that only checks the source ID in the document misses the display fault. A screenshot without the source manifest misses a wrong citation that is fully visible. Use both checks on one answer from the retrieval workshop. <a href="workshop-research.html">Retrieval and evaluation workshop</a></p>
<p>The Threads plugin and its service docs describe separate draft, publish and audit paths. The audit ranks the account's own posts with action counts divided by views and compares groups. Its read operation for public posts does not return the other accounts' post likes or views, according to the service docs. Those stated limits do not establish what a public web page might display. <a href="sources.html#DI05">[DI05]</a> <a href="sources.html#DI06">[DI06]</a></p>
<p>Apply the denominator rule to the research workshop. Suppose answer A receives 20 useful ratings from 1,000 exposures. Answer B gets 12 from 100. The counts favor A and the rates are 2% and 12% respectively. Keep the sample sizes visible. Fix the rubric and match task type before you compare. If the exposures are unknown, report the counts only. This calculation teaches the comparison and gives no result about Instagram, Threads or either retrieval system.</p>
<p>When you evaluate your own tutorial, choose two matched local tasks. Verify that a reader can find the exact input and repeat the stated test. Record one proof at the final surface and one check on the underlying data. <a href="prompt-pack/social-claim-test.md" download>Download the claim test prompt</a></p>
</section>
